<urn:atm:triage:finding/DAEMON-BOOTSTRAP-BOUNDARY-PLAN-1>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "DAEMON-BOOTSTRAP-BOUNDARY-PLAN-1" ;
  triage:title "DaemonBootstrapClient boundary drift leaves stale atm-daemon and atm-daemon-bootstrap dependents in the machine-readable contract" ;
  triage:description "The daemon-bootstrap boundary drift is between boundaries/atm-daemon-client/daemon-bootstrap.toml and docs/atm-daemon-client/boundaries.md, but the actual defect is in the TOML. The human-readable boundary and the accepted AC.8 plan both describe DaemonBootstrapClient as the thin-client bootstrap seam consumed by atm and atm-graft. The machine-readable record still allows atm-daemon and atm-daemon-bootstrap as dependents even though neither crate currently depends on atm-daemon-client and neither is recognized as a consumer in the prose boundary or the AC.8 relock plan. This is stale boundary widening, not a missing documentation update for legitimate live edges." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-AD" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "ARCH" ;
  triage:severity "important" ;
  triage:repeatable false ;
  triage:sweepScope "boundary record plus paired docs/plans" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:dispatchReady true ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-07-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/DAEMON-BOOTSTRAP-BOUNDARY-PLAN-1/integrate-AD/1> ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/DAEMON-BOOTSTRAP-BOUNDARY-PLAN-1/integrate-AD/2> ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/DAEMON-BOOTSTRAP-BOUNDARY-PLAN-1/integrate-AD/3> ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/DAEMON-BOOTSTRAP-BOUNDARY-PLAN-1/integrate-AD/4> ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/DAEMON-BOOTSTRAP-BOUNDARY-PLAN-1/integrate-AD/5> ;
  triage:openOn <urn:atm:triage:worktree/integrate-AD/develop-2026-07-10> ;
  triage:promoteTo <urn:atm:triage:worktree/integrate-AD/develop-2026-07-10> ;
  triage:notes """Root cause:

1. `boundaries/atm-daemon-client/daemon-bootstrap.toml:34` currently reads `allowed_dependents = ["atm", "atm-daemon", "atm-daemon-bootstrap", "atm-graft"]`. Both extra middle entries are stale.
2. The paired prose boundary never adopted either extra dependent. `docs/atm-daemon-client/boundaries.md:16-18,21-23,28-30` consistently describes the seam as the shared thin-client bootstrap path for `atm` and `atm-graft`.
3. The later AC.8 relock plan and closeout text also reaffirm that ownership: `docs/plans/phase-AC/sprint-AC8.md:97-110,123-135` and `docs/plans/phase-AC/plan-phase-AC.md:373-377` require `atm-daemon-client` to own the endpoint/bin bootstrap seam while `atm-graft` stops depending on `atm-daemon-bootstrap`.
4. The current code confirms there is no live `atm-daemon-bootstrap -> atm-daemon-client` edge to justify the widened dependent set. `crates/atm-daemon-bootstrap/Cargo.toml` depends only on `atm-core` and `atm-runtime`, and `crates/atm-daemon-bootstrap/src/lib.rs` only installs the retained runtime factory and forwards `with_default_roster_store()`.
5. The current code also confirms there is no live `atm-daemon -> atm-daemon-client` edge to justify the other widened dependent. The daemon does not appear as a consumer in `docs/atm-daemon-client/boundaries.md`, does not appear as a recognized consumer in the AC.8 relock plan, and the machine-readable contract already forbids the inverse owner edge `atm-daemon-client -> atm-daemon`, reinforcing that the bootstrap seam belongs to thin clients rather than the daemon runtime crate.

Conclusion:
- this is not an unresolved prose-doc omission
- this is a stale machine-readable allowance that survived after the bootstrap seam was relocked to the thin-client consumers `atm` and `atm-graft`
- both `atm-daemon` and `atm-daemon-bootstrap` are equally stale dependents and must be removed together

Remediation plan:

1. Tighten the machine-readable boundary:
   - change `boundaries/atm-daemon-client/daemon-bootstrap.toml` so `allowed_dependents = ["atm", "atm-graft"]`
   - keep `allowed_dependencies = ["atm-core", "atm-storage"]` and the existing forbidden edges unchanged unless a separate live-edge audit proves otherwise

2. Tighten the prose boundary to match the relocked ownership explicitly:
   - update `docs/atm-daemon-client/boundaries.md` to say the shared same-host bootstrap seam is consumed only by `atm` and `atm-graft`
   - add one explicit note that `atm-daemon-bootstrap` is not a consumer of this seam after AC.8; it remains a retained-runtime/roster transitional shim only

3. Tighten the planning artifacts so the same drift does not recur:
   - update `docs/plans/phase-AC/sprint-AC8.md` and `docs/plans/phase-AC/plan-phase-AC.md` only if they still imply `atm-daemon-bootstrap` is a legitimate dependent of DaemonBootstrapClient anywhere outside the historical migration narrative
   - otherwise leave the AC.8 narrative intact and cite it as the authority for the narrower dependent set

4. Add regression validation to the fix:
   - run `python3 .just/lint_boundaries.py`
   - run `cargo tree -p atm-daemon-client -e normal --prefix none`
   - run `! cargo tree -p atm-daemon-bootstrap -e normal --prefix none | rg "atm-daemon-client"`
   - run `rg -n "^allowed_dependents = \\[\\\"atm\\\", \\\"atm-graft\\\"\\]$" boundaries/atm-daemon-client/daemon-bootstrap.toml`
   - run `! rg -n "^allowed_dependents = .*atm-daemon.*|^allowed_dependents = .*atm-daemon-bootstrap.*" boundaries/atm-daemon-client/daemon-bootstrap.toml`
   - closure requires the machine-readable boundary to assert exactly `allowed_dependents = ["atm", "atm-graft"]`; substring checks against only `atm-daemon-bootstrap` are not sufficient because they would miss a surviving or reintroduced `atm-daemon` entry

5. Required lint-regression hardening:
   - extend `.just/lint_boundaries.py` or the equivalent boundary-lint path so `BOUNDARY-DaemonBootstrapClient` fails not only when a live Cargo edge is missing from `allowed_dependents`, but also when `allowed_dependents` contains over-permissive entries with no corresponding live edge
   - the hardening must concretely catch this defect class: a TOML that still lists `atm-daemon`, `atm-daemon-bootstrap`, or any other non-consumer crate in `allowed_dependents` must fail lint even if the boundary is otherwise permissive enough to satisfy the current under-permissive checks
   - do not leave this as advisory-only validation; the fix is not closed until this over-permissive dependent class is mechanically enforced

Files directly impacted by the fix:
- `boundaries/atm-daemon-client/daemon-bootstrap.toml`
- `docs/atm-daemon-client/boundaries.md`
- possibly `docs/plans/phase-AC/sprint-AC8.md`
- possibly `docs/plans/phase-AC/plan-phase-AC.md`
- `.just/lint_boundaries.py` or the equivalent boundary-lint implementation plus any direct regression tests covering that enforcement

What not to change:
- do not widen the prose boundary to include `atm-daemon` or `atm-daemon-bootstrap`; that would document non-existent live dependents and would undo the AC.8 relock
- do not move bootstrap helper ownership back into `atm-daemon-bootstrap`
- do not treat `atm-daemon-bootstrap`'s transitional retained-runtime/roster shim as evidence that it owns the thin-client daemon bootstrap seam""" .

